; =============================================================================
; Copy Propagation and Redundancy Elimination for E-Graph
; =============================================================================
;
; This module provides:
; - Copy propagation (replace copies with originals)
; - Common subexpression elimination (CSE) - inherent in e-graph
; - Value numbering - inherent in e-graph
; - Redundant load/store elimination
;
; Many of these optimizations are INHERENT to the e-graph approach:
; - CSE: Same expressions are automatically unified in e-graph
; - Value numbering: E-graph equivalence classes ARE value numbers
; - GVN: Global value numbering is the entire e-graph
;
; =============================================================================
; Copy Propagation
; =============================================================================
; When a value is copied (loaded then stored elsewhere), use original.

; Load from location that was just stored from another location
; This chains load-after-store forwarding
(rule ((= v (Load src mem))
       (= e (Load dst (StoreMem dst v mem))))
      ((union e v)))

; =============================================================================
; Redundant Load Elimination
; =============================================================================
; Multiple loads from same location in same memory state = same value

; Two loads from same pointer in same memory state
; (This is automatic in e-graph - same expression = same e-class)

; Load after store to DIFFERENT location = load from previous state
; (Requires alias analysis - conservative for now)

; =============================================================================
; Common Subexpression Elimination (CSE)
; =============================================================================
; CSE is AUTOMATIC in e-graphs!
; Any two expressions that are syntactically equal will be in the same e-class.
; The e-graph's union-find handles this.

; However, we can add rules to recognize semantically equivalent expressions:

; Commutative operations - already handled in arithmetic.egg
; a + b = b + a (creates equivalence)

; Reassociation - already handled
; (a + b) + c = a + (b + c)

; =============================================================================
; Algebraic Reassociation for Better CSE
; =============================================================================
; Normalize expressions to canonical form to improve CSE.

; Prefer constants on the right
; (Handled in arithmetic.egg with commutativity)

; Prefer lower-numbered symbols on the left for associative ops
; This is complex to express in egglog - rely on extraction cost

; =============================================================================
; Phi/Gamma Simplification (SSA Optimization)
; =============================================================================
; Gamma nodes that select the same value = that value (already in rvsdg.egg)

; Gamma where both branches compute same result
; Note: Gamma(BoolExpr, Expr, Expr) -> Expr - condition is BoolExpr
(rule ((= e (Gamma cond then_val then_val)))
      ((union e then_val)))

; Nested Gamma simplification
; Gamma(c1, Gamma(c2, a, b), Gamma(c2, a, b)) = Gamma(c2, a, b)
(rule ((= e (Gamma c1 (Gamma c2 a b) (Gamma c2 a b))))
      ((union e (Gamma c2 a b))))

; Gamma(c, Gamma(c, a, b), x) where c is same = Gamma(c, a, x)
; If outer condition is true, we take inner's true branch
(rule ((= e (Gamma c (Gamma c a b) x)))
      ((union e (Gamma c a x))))

; Gamma(c, x, Gamma(c, a, b)) where c is same = Gamma(c, x, b)
; If outer condition is false, we take inner's false branch
(rule ((= e (Gamma c x (Gamma c a b))))
      ((union e (Gamma c x b))))

; =============================================================================
; Predicated Execution Optimization
; =============================================================================
; Convert conditional code to predicated operations when beneficial.

; Gamma selecting between x and x+1 based on condition
; This might be better as: x + (cond ? 1 : 0)
; Note: Gamma(BoolExpr, Expr, Expr) -> Expr, Add(IntExpr, IntExpr) -> IntExpr
; These rules work when x is Expr and the Add/Const are wrapped with IntToExpr
; Note: Const returns IntExpr, wrapped with IntToExpr for Expr context in Gamma args
(rule ((= e (Gamma cond (IntToExpr (Add (ExprToInt x) (Const 1))) x)))
      ((union e (IntToExpr (Add (ExprToInt x) (GammaI cond (Const 1) (Const 0)))))))
(rule ((= e (Gamma cond x (IntToExpr (Add (ExprToInt x) (Const 1))))))
      ((union e (IntToExpr (Add (ExprToInt x) (GammaI cond (Const 0) (Const 1)))))))

; =============================================================================
; Partial Redundancy Elimination (PRE)
; =============================================================================
; PRE moves computations to make them fully redundant.
; In e-graph, this is handled by finding equivalent expressions.

; The key insight: if an expression is computed on some paths but not others,
; we can move it earlier (to dominator) to compute it on all paths.
; This is implicitly handled by the e-graph's global view.

; =============================================================================
; Additional Redundancy Elimination Patterns
; =============================================================================
; NOTE: Most redundancy elimination patterns are already defined elsewhere:
; - FAbs(FAbs(x)) = FAbs(x) is in floating_point.egg
; - FMin(x, x) = x is in floating_point.egg
; - SMin(x, x) = x is in logical.egg
; - UMin(x, x) = x is in logical.egg
; - SMax(x, x) = x, UMax(x, x) = x are in logical.egg
; - Sign(Sign(x)) = Sign(x) is in glsl.egg
; - SAbs(SAbs(x)) = SAbs(x) is in glsl.egg
; - Floor/Ceil/Trunc/Round idempotence is in glsl.egg
; - FClamp(FClamp(...)) = FClamp(...) is in glsl.egg
; - SClamp(SClamp(...)) = SClamp(...) is in glsl.egg
; - UClamp(UClamp(...)) = UClamp(...) is in glsl.egg
; - NMin(x, x) = x, NMax(x, x) = x are in type_conversion.egg
; - Nested min/max absorption is in logical.egg
;
; The rules below are for patterns NOT defined elsewhere.

; NClamp idempotence (NaN-ignoring clamp, not covered elsewhere)
; NClamp(NClamp(x, lo, hi), lo, hi) = NClamp(x, lo, hi)
; Note: NClamp(FloatExpr, FloatExpr, FloatExpr) -> FloatExpr
(rule ((= e (NClamp (NClamp x lo hi) lo hi)))
      ((union e (NClamp x lo hi))))
